## 3. Backend API Contract (AUTHORITATIVE)

Verified against `LearningEdge/src/LearningEdge.Api` and `LearningEdge.Application`.

### 3.1 Base URLs

| Environment | HTTP | HTTPS |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `dotnet run` (Development) | `http://localhost:5087` | `https://localhost:7176` |
| `docker compose up` (Docker) | `http://localhost:5000` | `https://localhost:5001` |

Put the base URL in `environments/`. Never hardcode it in a service. The API calls
`UseHttpsRedirection()`, so prefer the HTTPS origin.

### 3.2 Route shape

Routes are URL-path versioned and the controller segment is **singular and lowercase**:

```text
/api/v{version}/{controller}      →  /api/v1/user      /api/v1/organization
```

Do **not** generate `/api/users` or `/api/organizations`. Only API version `1.0` is
implemented; a `v2` OpenAPI document is registered but has no endpoints. OpenAPI JSON is at
`/openapi/v1.json` and Swagger UI is served only in Development.

### 3.3 The response envelope — every endpoint returns `ApiResult<T>`

All handlers return `Result<T>`, which is serialized with ASP.NET Core's default camelCase
policy:

```ts
export interface ApiResult<T> {
  success: boolean;
  error: string;     // '' when success is true
  data: T | null;
}
```

**Critical behavioural notes:**

- A missing record returns **HTTP 200 with `success: false`**, not 404. The controllers'
  `if (result == null) return NotFound()` check can never fire, because `Result<T>` is a
  class instance that is never null. **Always branch on `success`, never on HTTP 404.**
- `POST` endpoints return **`ApiResult<string>` containing only the new GUID** — not the
  created DTO. The controllers' declared `ActionResult<UserDTO>` return type is inaccurate.
  After a create, re-fetch by id if you need the full record.
- Centralize unwrapping in `core/http/api.service.ts`. Feature code must never touch
  `.data` or `.success` directly.

### 3.4 Implemented endpoints — this is the complete list

| Method | Path | Request body | `data` payload |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| POST | `/api/v1/user` | `CreateUserCommand` | `string` (new user GUID) |
| GET | `/api/v1/user/{id}` | — | `UserDTO` — **rate limited** |
| POST | `/api/v1/organization` | `CreateOrganizationCommand` | `string` (new org GUID) |
| GET | `/api/v1/organization/{id}` | — | `OrganizationDTO` |

**There are no list, search, update, or delete endpoints anywhere in the solution.** Every
listing screen in Section 2 must therefore be built mock-first (Section 3.7).

### 3.5 Verified DTO and command shapes

```ts
export interface UserDTO {
  id: string;             // Guid
  firstName: string;
  lastName: string;
  email: string;          // plain string; the Email value object is not used by User
  role: UserRole;         // NUMBER — see 3.6
  organizationId: string; // Guid
}

export interface OrganizationDTO {
  id: string;             // Guid
  name: string;
  description: string;    // defaults to '' server-side
}

export interface CreateUserCommand {
  firstName: string;
  lastName: string;
  email: string;
  role: UserRole;
  organizationId: string;
}

export interface CreateOrganizationCommand {
  name: string;
  description: string;
}
```

`Organization` has **only** `name` and `description`. Do not generate address, phone,
logo, or contact fields — the API will ignore them. (`Address` and `Email` value objects
exist in the domain layer but are not wired to either entity.)

### 3.6 Roles are NUMERIC — use the backend names

`UserRole` is an int enum. The `[EnumMember]` attributes on it are a `DataContractSerializer`
concept that `System.Text.Json` ignores, and **no `JsonStringEnumConverter` is registered**.
The API therefore emits `"role": 3`, not `"role": "OrgAdmin"`.

```ts
export enum UserRole {
  Learner    = 1,
  Instructor = 2,
  OrgAdmin   = 3,
  SysAdmin   = 4,
}
```

Use these four identifiers everywhere in guards, stores, templates, and route metadata. Map
to friendly labels only at the view layer, via a `roleLabel` pipe:

| `UserRole` | Display label | Scope |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `SysAdmin` (4) | Super Admin | Platform — all organizations |
| `OrgAdmin` (3) | Admin | Single organization |
| `Instructor` (2) | Staff | Single organization |
| `Learner` (1) | Trainee | Single organization, own records |

### 3.7 Frontend-owned domains (no backend yet)

`Training`, `Category`, `TrainingAssignment`, `TrainingResult`, `Message`, and
`ProcessingJob` do not exist server-side. For each one:

1. Define the interface in `core/models/`.
2. Define an abstract repository (e.g. `TrainingRepository`) in `core/services/`.
3. Provide an in-memory mock implementation, selected via an environment flag
   (`useMockApi: true`).
4. Leave a real HTTP implementation stubbed so it can be swapped without touching features.

Mark every such screen with a `// MOCK: no backend endpoint` comment so the gap stays visible.

### 3.7.1 Sample data lives in JSON, not in TypeScript

All mock seed data lives in `public/mock-data/tms-sample-data.json` — one top-level
collection per future endpoint (`organizations`, `users`, `categories`, `trainings`,
`assignments`, `results`, `messages`, `processingJobs`), each holding the raw `data`
payload rather than a `{ success, error, data }` envelope.

- `MockDataLoader` fetches it with `HttpClient` at bootstrap via `provideAppInitializer`,
  registered only when `useMockApi` is true. The fetch is marked with the
  `SKIP_API_CONCERNS` HTTP context token so the auth and retry interceptors ignore it.
- `MockDb` holds no literals. It starts empty and is populated by `seed(dataset)`, so
  changing what any page shows is a JSON edit, never a code change.
- `mock-data.parser.ts` validates every field and names the offending path on failure.
  A failed or malformed load is logged and the app still boots — screens show their empty
  state rather than a white page.
- Date fields accept either an ISO string or a relative offset (`+14d`, `-3d`, `0d`),
  resolved at load time so the samples never go stale.
- Each mock credential's `userId` must match a seeded user, otherwise the signed-in
  identity owns no rows and the learner and instructor screens render empty.

`mock-data.parser.spec.ts` parses the shipped file and asserts referential integrity
(tenant scoping, assignment and training links, pass/fail consistency). Keep those
assertions passing when editing the sample data.

### 3.8 Rate limiting returns 503, not 429

`GET /api/v1/user/{id}` carries `[EnableRateLimiting("fixed")]`: a fixed window of
**5 requests per 10 seconds** with a queue limit of 2. Because `RejectionStatusCode` is not
configured, ASP.NET Core rejects with **HTTP 503 Service Unavailable** (the framework
default), *not* 429. `retry.interceptor.ts` must retry 503 with exponential backoff and
jitter, cap the attempts, and surface a toast on final failure. Do not assume a
`Retry-After` header is present.

### 3.9 Two blockers to flag, not work around

- **No CORS policy exists in the API.** `Program.cs` registers none. A browser app on a
  different origin cannot call this API at all. Do not attempt a client-side workaround or
  a dev proxy that masks it — report it so CORS gets added server-side.
- **No authentication is registered.** The pipeline calls `UseAuthorization()` with no
  `UseAuthentication()` and no scheme, so bearer tokens are accepted-and-ignored. Mock mode
  is the only functional path.

