## 7. UI/UX, Styling & Accessibility

- **Styling:** Tailwind CSS utility classes for layout and spacing, plus component-scoped
  SCSS for anything Tailwind cannot express. Define the palette, spacing, and typography as
  design tokens in `styles.scss`; never hardcode hex values in a component.
- **Layout:** persistent collapsible sidebar, sticky top navbar with organization context
  and user menu, and a scrollable main content region. Collapse the sidebar to an overlay
  drawer below 768px.
- **Every data view must implement four states:** loading (skeleton, not a bare spinner),
  empty (explanatory copy plus primary action), error (message plus retry), and populated.
  A screen missing any of these is incomplete.
- **Accessibility is not optional:** semantic landmarks (`nav`, `main`, `aside`), a visible
  focus ring on every interactive element, labelled form controls tied via `for`/`id`,
  `aria-live="polite"` on the toast container, full keyboard operation of the sidebar, org
  switcher, and modals, focus trapping and restoration in modals, `Escape` to dismiss, and
  WCAG 2.1 AA contrast. Never convey status by colour alone — pair it with text or an icon.
- **Tables:** use `scope` on headers and a caption or `aria-label`. Provide client-side
  sorting and pagination, since the API offers neither.

---

## 8. Forms, Validation, Errors & Notifications

- Use **typed reactive forms** (`FormGroup<{...}>` with `NonNullableFormBuilder`). No
  template-driven forms, no `any`.
- Mirror server constraints: `firstName` and `email` are required (the API throws
  `ArgumentNullException` on null), `lastName` is optional, `role` must be a valid
  `UserRole`, `organizationId` must be a GUID, and `Organization.name` is required.
- Validate email format client-side; the server stores it as a plain string without checks.
- Show validation messages only after a control is touched or the form is submitted.
  Disable submit while in flight and prevent double submission.
- **Error handling:** a single `ToastService` backed by a signal array is the only way to
  surface errors. Map failures consistently — `success: false` shows `error` from the
  envelope, 503 is handled by the retry interceptor and reported only after retries are
  exhausted, 0 or a CORS failure reads "Cannot reach the server", and 5xx reads
  "Something went wrong. Please try again."
- Never swallow an error silently and never log a raw error object to the console in
  production code paths.

---

## 9. Document Processing Pipeline

`features/processing/` is **not** a simple upload form. Per the high-level design in
`miocroservices_to_implement.docx`, uploading a `.docx` or `.pdf` starts a long-running,
multi-stage backend job: parse the document, store paragraphs and embeddings, generate
questionnaires, then notify the web app on completion.

Build it accordingly:

- Drag-and-drop plus a keyboard-accessible file input. Validate extension and size
  **before** upload and show per-file progress.
- Model the job as `ProcessingJob { id, fileName, stage, progress, status, error }` with
  stages `uploading`, `parsing`, `storing`, `generating`, `complete`, `failed`.
- Track jobs in a `ProcessingStore` so progress survives navigation away from the screen.
- Poll job status on an interval with backoff, behind a `ProcessingRepository` abstraction
  so it can later be swapped for SignalR or WebSocket push without touching components.
- Handle partial failure: one failed file must not fail the batch. Offer per-file retry.
- All of this is mock-first today — no orchestrator endpoint exists.

